(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-1-chemistry-panels-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Chemistry Panels, Renal Function & Electrolytes Quiz 2 — PDM I Exam 1","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What general lesson applies to chemistry results?","choices":["A lab value narrows the differential and directs the next test rather than being diagnostic by itself","A lab value is diagnostic on its own once it falls outside the reference range","A lab value should always be confirmed by imaging before acting","A lab value is only useful when the whole panel is abnormal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a chemistry result shifts the probability of the diagnoses under consideration and tells you what to order next, rather than settling the question on its own. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 24 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"Besides buffering, what is bicarbonate's other stated function?","choices":["Determining extracellular osmolality","Helping transport carbon dioxide in the bloodstream","Helping transport oxygen to the tissues","Maintaining the resting membrane potential across the cell"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — bicarbonate is the primary extracellular buffer, maintaining acid-base balance, and it also helps transport carbon dioxide in the bloodstream. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Besides the liver, in which four tissues is aspartate aminotransferase found?","choices":["Cardiac muscle, skeletal muscle, kidney, and brain","Bone, placenta, intestine, and kidney","Spleen, pancreas, lung, and thyroid","Red blood cells, platelets, bone marrow, and lymph nodes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — aspartate aminotransferase is also found in cardiac and skeletal muscle, kidney and brain, so it is less liver-specific than alanine transaminase. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A raised anion gap suggests what?","choices":["A respiratory rather than a metabolic problem","An increase in unmeasured acids","A loss of bicarbonate from the gut or kidney","An excess of measured cations"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a wide gap means unmeasured anions have accumulated, as with lactate, ketoacids or an ingested toxin. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 22 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"How does loss of chloride, as in vomiting, produce a metabolic alkalosis?","choices":["It lowers the strong ion difference","It directly removes hydrogen ions from the blood","It stimulates the lungs to retain carbon dioxide","It raises the strong ion difference"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — chloride is a strong anion, so losing it in gastric fluid widens the strong ion difference, and electroneutrality is restored by a rise in bicarbonate. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How is the anion gap adjusted when albumin is low?","choices":["Correct it by 1.6 for every one gram per decilitre","Correct it upward by about 2.5 for every one gram per decilitre the albumin has fallen","Correct it downward by about 2.5 for every one gram per decilitre the albumin has fallen","No correction is needed, because albumin is not charged"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a low albumin calls for correcting the anion gap by about 2.5 for every one gram per deciliter drop in albumin. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What distinguishes a chem-7 from a chem-8?","choices":["A chem-8 includes calcium and a chem-7 does not","A chem-8 includes albumin and a chem-7 does not","A chem-8 includes magnesium and a chem-7 does not","A chem-8 includes bicarbonate and a chem-7 does not"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — calcium is the difference between the two older names. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A chemistry panel is best described as a snapshot of which three things?","choices":["Nutrition, hydration, and body composition","Chemical balance, metabolism, and organ function","Infection, inflammation, and immune response","Oxygen delivery, oxygen consumption, and tissue perfusion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the panel captures a single moment, showing whether the internal chemical environment is balanced, whether fuel is being handled normally, and whether the organs that maintain both are working. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Besides urea nitrogen and creatinine, what else appears in the renal pattern?","choices":["A raised phosphate, a lowered calcium, and albuminuria","A raised calcium, a lowered phosphate, and glycosuria","A raised albumin with a lowered total protein","A raised bilirubin with a lowered alkaline phosphatase"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the renal pattern adds a raised potassium and phosphate, a lowered calcium, metabolic acidosis and albuminuria to the rising urea nitrogen and creatinine. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Besides renal excretion, what is the other mechanism regulating potassium?","choices":["Loss through sweat and insensible routes","Transcellular shifts driven by insulin, acid-base status, and catecholamines","Storage in, and release from, the glycogen held in skeletal muscle","Binding to and release from plasma albumin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — potassium is regulated by renal excretion through aldosterone and by transcellular shifts driven by insulin, acid-base status and catecholamines. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 10 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"In the stepwise approach to an abnormal comprehensive metabolic panel, which step comes first?","choices":["The kidney group — urea nitrogen, creatinine, and the filtration rate","The minerals — calcium","The electrolytes and acid-base group, then calculate the anion gap","The liver group — the transaminases, alkaline phosphatase, and bilirubin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the stepwise read starts with sodium, potassium, chloride, bicarbonate and the anion gap, then renal, glucose, liver and finally calcium. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What are the two causes of isolated hyperbilirubinaemia?","choices":["Viral hepatitis and drug injury","Alcohol and fatty liver disease","Gilbert syndrome and haemolysis","Gallstones and primary biliary cholangitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — Gilbert syndrome and hemolysis raise the bilirubin while the enzymes stay normal, which is the isolated hyperbilirubinemia pattern. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 17 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"Which test confirms that a raised alkaline phosphatase is of hepatic origin?","choices":["Lactate dehydrogenase","A bone-specific isoenzyme assay","A repeat alkaline phosphatase in one month","Gamma-glutamyl transferase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — alkaline phosphatase is also found in bone, placenta and intestine, so gamma-glutamyl transferase is used to confirm a hepatic origin. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which four non-renal factors raise blood urea nitrogen?","choices":["Dehydration, gastrointestinal bleeding, high protein intake, and catabolic states","Dehydration, haemolysis, low protein intake, and pregnancy","Liver failure, malnutrition, overhydration, and immobility","Muscle injury, strenuous exercise, high creatine intake, and male sex"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — dehydration, gastrointestinal bleeding, high protein intake and catabolic states raise urea nitrogen, so it is non-specific and read with creatinine. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Serum sodium and total-body sodium reflect different things. Which pairing is correct?","choices":["Serum sodium reflects water balance; total-body sodium governs extracellular volume","Serum sodium reflects total-body sodium; water balance governs extracellular volume","Both serum sodium and total-body sodium reflect water balance","Serum sodium reflects extracellular volume; water balance governs total-body sodium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the concentration tells you about water, while the total quantity of sodium in the body determines how much extracellular fluid there is to hold it. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 9 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"Following from how a normal range is defined, what does a normal result mean?","choices":["A normal value effectively excludes disease","A normal value should always be repeated","A normal value means the sample was handled correctly","A normal value does not exclude disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the range is set to capture most healthy people, not to separate sick from well, so a patient with disease can sit inside it just as a healthy person can sit outside. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A patient's sodium is 138, chloride 104 and bicarbonate 24 milliequivalents per litre. How should the anion gap be read?","choices":["It is 10, which is above the normal range","It is 14, which is above the normal range","It is 34, which is markedly raised","It is 10, which sits within the normal range"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — 138 minus the sum of 104 and 24 is 10, which sits inside the standard normal range of 8 to 12 milliequivalents per liter. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Abnormal liver tests should be followed by which imaging first?","choices":["Magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography","Plain abdominal radiography","Ultrasound","Computed tomography with contrast"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — abnormal liver tests lead to imaging that starts with ultrasound for steatosis and biliary dilation, then computed tomography or magnetic resonance. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Why is creatinine a more specific marker of kidney function than blood urea nitrogen?","choices":["It is produced at a rate that never varies between people, whatever their build or age","It is measured by a more accurate laboratory method","It rises earlier in kidney injury than any other marker","It is filtered by the kidneys and rises as the filtration rate falls, without the many non-renal influences on urea nitrogen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — creatinine is filtered by the kidneys and rises as filtration falls, while urea nitrogen is also raised by dehydration, bleeding, protein intake and catabolic states. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A patient has a low chloride with a high bicarbonate. Which acid-base picture does that suggest?","choices":["Metabolic acidosis with a raised anion gap","Respiratory acidosis","A normal anion gap acidosis from bicarbonate loss","Metabolic alkalosis, such as from vomiting"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — chloride and bicarbonate move reciprocally to preserve electroneutrality, so a low chloride with a high bicarbonate suggests metabolic alkalosis, as from vomiting. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 11 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"A ratio of blood urea nitrogen to creatinine above twenty to one suggests which kind of cause?","choices":["A postrenal obstructive cause","A hepatic cause","A prerenal cause","An intrinsic renal cause"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a ratio above 20 to 1 points to a prerenal cause such as hypovolemia, distinguishing it from an intrinsic renal cause. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"On a comprehensive metabolic panel, what does the abbreviation ALP stand for?","choices":["Alanine transaminase","Alkaline phosphatase","Aspartate aminotransferase","Albumin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — ALP (alkaline phosphatase) is one of the six liver and protein markers the comprehensive panel adds, and it marks cholestasis or bile duct obstruction. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Why do insulin and acid-base status matter specifically when reading diabetic ketoacidosis results?","choices":["Both interfere chemically with the potassium assay","Both shift potassium across the cell membrane, so the serum value can mislead","Both raise the measured potassium by causing haemolysis of the sample","Both reduce renal potassium excretion to near zero"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — insulin and acidosis shift potassium across cell membranes, so in ketoacidosis the serum potassium may be raised while total-body potassium is depleted. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 10 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"Which cluster of symptoms is an indication for a chemistry panel?","choices":["Fatigue, weakness, oedema, jaundice, confusion, nausea and vomiting","Fever, night sweats, weight loss, and lymph node swelling","Chest pain, breathlessness, and palpitations","Joint pain, rash, and morning stiffness"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — fatigue, weakness, edema, jaundice, confusion, nausea and vomiting are the symptom indication, alongside screening, medications, monitoring and acute illness. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What does serum osmolality allow you to distinguish?","choices":["Raised-gap from normal-gap acidosis","Acute from chronic kidney disease","True hypotonic hyponatraemia from the pseudo- and hypertonic forms","Prerenal failure from intrinsic renal failure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — osmolality is normal in pseudohyponatraemia and raised in the hypertonic form, so it isolates the true hypotonic case. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What distinguishes pseudohyponatraemia from a true hypotonic hyponatraemia?","choices":["The osmolality is normal in pseudohyponatraemia","The osmolality is low in pseudohyponatraemia","The potassium is also low in pseudohyponatraemia","The urine sodium is above 40 in pseudohyponatraemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — in pseudohyponatremia severe hyperlipidemia or hyperproteinemia gives a falsely low sodium while the osmolality stays normal. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"After three days of intractable vomiting, what does the panel show?","choices":["Raised sodium, raised potassium, and a normal bicarbonate","Normal electrolytes with an isolated raised anion gap","Low sodium, low potassium, low chloride, and a raised bicarbonate with alkalaemia","Low sodium, raised potassium, raised chloride, and a low bicarbonate with acidaemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the case shows low sodium, potassium and chloride with a raised bicarbonate and alkalemia, maintained by volume, potassium and chloride depletion. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Besides the pancreatic hormones, which organ contributes to glucose regulation, and how?","choices":["The thyroid, through its effect on metabolic rate","The adrenal medulla, through glycogen storage","The kidney, through gluconeogenesis","The spleen, through glucose storage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — glucose is balanced by insulin and by glucagon and the counter-regulatory hormones, and the kidney also contributes through gluconeogenesis. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which complication follows BOTH high and low potassium?","choices":["Seizures","Acute kidney injury","Hepatic encephalopathy with confusion","Life-threatening cardiac arrhythmias"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — potassium maintains membrane electrical potential, and both hyperkalemia and hypokalemia can cause life-threatening arrhythmias. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 10 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"How do the kidneys handle potassium?","choices":["It is reabsorbed in exchange for sodium in the proximal tubule","It is excreted by the kidneys with no reabsorption","It is filtered and then almost entirely reabsorbed","It is neither filtered nor secreted, and is lost only in stool"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — potassium is excreted by the kidneys with no reabsorption, so it must be replaced by diet or supplementation or it can drop rapidly. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 10"}]};
